{"name":"A Certain Shade of Green","hex":"#d1edee","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"certain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Sure in one's mind, positive; absolutely confident in the truth of something.","Not to be doubted or denied; established as a fact."]},{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["Having been determined but not specified."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/certain"},{"term":"shade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Darkness where light, particularly sunlight, is blocked.","Something that blocks light, particularly in a window."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To shield (someone or something) from light.","To shield oneself from light."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shade"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"S.C.I.E.N.C.E.","blocks":[{"text":"S.C.I.E.N.C.E. is the second studio album by American rock band Incubus. It was released on September 9, 1997, by Epic and Immortal Records. The album was certified gold by the RIAA, and is the second and final release to feature Gavin Koppel, who first appeared on the 1997 Enjoy Incubus EP. It has been occasionally considered the band's proper debut album, due to the nature of their independent release Fungus Amongus."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4f/Incubus_Science.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S.C.I.E.N.C.E."},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green"},{"title":"Certainty","blocks":[{"text":"Certainty is the epistemic property of beliefs which a person has no rational grounds for doubting. One standard way of defining epistemic certainty is that a belief is certain if and only if the person holding that belief could not be mistaken in holding that belief. Other common definitions of certainty involve the indubitable nature of such beliefs or define certainty as a property of those beliefs with the greatest possible justification. Certainty is closely related to knowledge, although contemporary philosophers tend to treat knowledge as having lower requirements than certainty."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Certainty"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Santana_1969_press_photo.jpg/500px-Santana_1969_press_photo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Santana_1969_press_photo.jpg/1280px-Santana_1969_press_photo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Santana_1969_press_photo.jpg/500px-Santana_1969_press_photo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rolling Stone compared \"Summer Romance\" to the music of Santana[35]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASantana_1969_press_photo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/RGB_illumination.jpg/330px-RGB_illumination.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/RGB_illumination.jpg/330px-RGB_illumination.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Red, green and blue lights, representing the three basic additive primary colors of the RGB color system, red, green, and blue.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARGB_illumination.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/500px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w